          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long does broadcast copyright last in India?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Broadcast reproduction right lasts 25 years from the beginning of the calendar year following the broadcast year. A broadcast in 2026 is protected until 31 December 2051. This is separate from content copyright which lasts author's lifetime plus 60 years for most works."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is Section 37 of the Copyright Act?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Section 37 grants every broadcasting organisation a 'broadcast reproduction right' in respect of its broadcasts. It defines the 25-year duration, lists exclusive rights (re-broadcasting, public performance for payment, recording, reproducing, selling/renting), and specifies that performing these without license is infringement."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can podcasts be protected under broadcast copyright?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Whether podcast distribution qualifies as 'broadcasting' under Section 2(dd) is legally evolving. However, podcast producers can protect content through copyright registration of sound recordings and literary/musical works, and establish clear usage terms through licensing agreements. Patron advises Pune podcast producers on optimal strategy."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
