<!-- SECTION 2: OVERVIEW / INTRO + TL;DR -->
<section class="content-section" id="overview-section">
    <div class="content-container">
        <div class="text-content">
            <h2 class="section-title">Copyright for Broadcast in Pune</h2>
            <div class="content-text">
                <div class="highlight-box" style="margin-bottom:20px;">
                    <p><strong>&#128204; TL;DR - Copyright for Broadcast in Pune Services at a Glance</strong></p>
                    <p>Broadcast reproduction right under Section 37 of the Copyright Act 1957 is a special right granted to every broadcasting organisation protecting their broadcast signal from unauthorised re-broadcasting, recording, and exploitation. Lasts 25 years. Distinct from content copyright - the signal is protected, not the content. Pune's broadcasting ecosystem includes FM radio (Radio Mirchi, Radio City, Red FM), Marathi TV production (Zee Marathi, Star Pravah), OTT/streaming, podcasts, FTII graduates, and corporate AV producers.</p><div class="table-responsive-wrapper" style="margin-top:16px;"><table><thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Legal Framework</td><td>Copyright Act 1957 Chapter VIII (Sections 37-39A); Section 37 (broadcast reproduction right); Section 2(dd); Section 31D (statutory license)</td></tr><tr><td>Right Holder</td><td>Every broadcasting organisation - radio, TV, satellite, cable, and (debated) internet broadcasters</td></tr><tr><td>Duration</td><td>25 years from beginning of calendar year following broadcast year</td></tr><tr><td>Protected Against</td><td>Re-broadcasting, recording, reproducing recordings, selling/renting recordings - without license</td></tr><tr><td>Does NOT Protect</td><td>The content in the broadcast (that has separate copyright). Signal is what is protected</td></tr><tr><td>Statutory License</td><td>Section 31D allows broadcasters to use published works by paying royalties</td></tr><tr><td>Infringement Remedies</td><td>Civil (injunction, damages - Section 55); Criminal (up to 3 years, Rs 2 lakh fine - Section 63)</td></tr></tbody></table></div></p>
                </div>
                <p>Pune has a diverse and growing broadcasting ecosystem. FM radio stations (Radio Mirchi, Radio City, Red FM), Marathi TV production houses (Zee Marathi, Star Pravah, Sony Marathi), FTII (Film and Television Institute of India), the Pune International Film Festival (PIFF), podcast producers, OTT platforms, and corporate AV producers. Learn more about <a href="/copyright-registration-for-broadcast">Copyright for Broadcast across India</a>.</p>
                <p>For all these entities, understanding and protecting broadcast reproduction rights - alongside content copyright - is essential for monetisation, licensing, and piracy enforcement. Patron Accounting's Pune office provides broadcast copyright advisory, content registration, statutory license (Section 31D) compliance, licensing agreements, and anti-piracy enforcement.</p>
                <p>Content is reviewed quarterly for accuracy.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 3: WHAT IS GSTAT APPEAL FILING -->
<section class="content-section" id="what-section">
    <div class="content-container">
        <div class="two-column">
            <div class="column-content">
                <h2 class="section-title">What Is Copyright for Broadcast?</h2>
                <div class="content-text what-is-definition">
                    
                    <p>Copyright for broadcast, technically 'broadcast reproduction right', is a special right under Section 37 of the Copyright Act 1957 granted to every broadcasting organisation. Section 2(dd) defines 'broadcast' as communication to the public by wireless diffusion or by wire, including re-broadcast. This right protects the broadcast signal itself - distinct from the copyright in the content being transmitted.</p><p>The right gives the broadcasting organisation exclusive control over re-broadcasting, public performance for payment, making recordings, reproducing recordings, and selling/renting recordings. Infringement carries civil remedies (injunction, damages) and criminal penalties (up to 3 years, Rs 2 lakh fine).</p><p>Critically, Section 39 provides that broadcast reproduction right does not affect the separate copyright in the underlying content. This creates a layered protection system: a Pune FM station has broadcast right over its signal, while the songs it transmits have their own separate copyright.</p>

                </div>
                <div class="highlight-box" style="margin-top:20px;">
                    <p><strong>Key Terms for Copyright for Broadcast in Pune:</strong></p>
                    <ul><li><strong>Section 37 (Broadcast Reproduction Right):</strong> 25-year protection for broadcast signals. Automatic for every broadcasting organisation</li><li><strong>Section 31D (Statutory License):</strong> Broadcasters can use published works by paying prescribed royalties without separate permission</li><li><strong>Section 39 (Coexistence):</strong> Broadcast right and content copyright coexist - protecting the same broadcast from two angles</li><li><strong>Section 2(dd):</strong> Broadcast = communication to public by wireless diffusion or wire, including re-broadcast</li><li><strong>Section 38 (Performer's Rights):</strong> Performers have special rights that coexist with broadcast rights</li></ul>

                </div>
            </div>
