            <div class="faq-expanded">
                <aside class="faq-expanded__aside">
                    <h2 class="faq-expanded__title">Frequently Asked Questions - Broadcast Copyright in Pune</h2>
                    <p class="faq-expanded__lead">Answers about Section 37 broadcast reproduction right, statutory license, content vs signal copyright, podcasts, OTT, and enforcement for Pune broadcasters.</p>
                    @include('partials.faq-enquiry-form', ['enquiryLocation' => 'Pune', 'enquiryService' => 'Broadcast Copyright in Pune'])
                </aside>
                <div class="faq-expanded__list">
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-1">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">What is broadcast reproduction right?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Broadcast reproduction right is a special right under Section 37 of the Copyright Act 1957 granted to every broadcasting organisation. It protects the broadcast signal from unauthorised re-broadcasting, recording, reproduction, and commercial exploitation. This right lasts 25 years from the calendar year following the broadcast.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-2">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">How to protect broadcast copyright in Pune?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Broadcast reproduction right under Section 37 is automatic. To maximise protection: register copyright for underlying content on copyright.gov.in, draft proper licensing agreements, ensure Section 31D statutory license compliance, execute copyright assignments from creators, and establish anti-piracy monitoring. Patron handles all from Wagholi.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-3">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">How long does broadcast copyright last in India?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Broadcast reproduction right lasts 25 years from the beginning of the calendar year following the broadcast year. A broadcast in 2026 is protected until 31 December 2051. This is separate from content copyright which lasts author's lifetime plus 60 years for most works.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-4">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">What is Section 37 of the Copyright Act?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Section 37 grants every broadcasting organisation a 'broadcast reproduction right' in respect of its broadcasts. It defines the 25-year duration, lists exclusive rights (re-broadcasting, public performance for payment, recording, reproducing, selling/renting), and specifies that performing these without license is infringement.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-5">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">Can podcasts be protected under broadcast copyright?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Whether podcast distribution qualifies as 'broadcasting' under Section 2(dd) is legally evolving. However, podcast producers can protect content through copyright registration of sound recordings and literary/musical works, and establish clear usage terms through licensing agreements. Patron advises Pune podcast producers on optimal strategy.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-6">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">What is statutory license under Section 31D?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Section 31D allows any broadcasting organisation to communicate published literary/musical works and sound recordings by paying prescribed royalties - no separate permission from each copyright owner needed. A Pune radio station can play songs via statutory license. Compliance documentation and payment records must be maintained.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-7">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">What acts constitute infringement of broadcast rights?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Under Section 37(3), without the broadcaster's license: re-broadcasting the broadcast, causing public hearing/viewing on payment, making sound/visual recordings, reproducing such recordings, and selling/renting recordings commercially. Civil remedies (injunction, damages) and criminal penalties (up to 3 years, Rs 2 lakh fine) available.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-8">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">What is the difference between content and broadcast copyright?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Content copyright (Section 14) protects the creative work - song, script, film. Broadcast copyright (Section 37) protects the broadcast signal/transmission. They coexist under Section 39. A TV channel's broadcast of a Marathi film involves both: film copyright (producer) and signal right (channel). Both layers must be protected.</p></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>


            <!-- Quick Answers -->
            <div class="highlight-box" style="margin-top:32px;">
                <p><strong>Quick Answers</strong></p>
                <p><strong>Broadcast reproduction right kya hota hai?</strong> Section 37 ke under broadcasting organisation ko milta hai. Broadcast signal ko protect karta hai. 25 saal tak valid hai.</p><p><strong>Radio station ko copyright registration karni chahiye kya?</strong> Broadcast signal ka right automatic hai (Section 37). Lekin jo content broadcast kar rahe ho - gaane, shows, jingles - unka copyright register karna chahiye. Dono saath mein karo.</p><p><strong>Kya OTT platform ko broadcast copyright milta hai?</strong> Legally debatable hai. Content copyright register karni chahiye aur proper licensing agreements rakhne chahiye. Patron sab advise karta hai.</p>

            </div>
